Q: How can I detect and visualize multiple touch points with JavaScript? A: <p>key everything off `touch.identifier` and keep your own map:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>
const active = new Map();

const put = e =\&gt; { for (const t of e.changedTouches) active.set(t.identifier, {x: t.clientX, y: t.clientY}); draw(); };

const drop = e =\&gt; { for (const t of e.changedTouches) active.delete(t.identifier); draw(); };

el.addEventListener('touchstart', put, {passive: false});

el.addEventListener('touchmove', e =\&gt; { e.preventDefault(); put(e); }, {passive: false});

el.addEventListener('touchend', drop);

el.addEventListener('touchcancel', drop);

</code></pre>
<p>use `changedTouches` in the handlers - thats only the fingers involved in that specific event, which is what you want for add/update/remove. `touches` is every finger on the screen, `targetTouches` is every finger on that element; both are fine if you'd rather just rebuild the whole set each frame from `e.touches`.</p>
<p>also set `touch-action: none` on the element or the browser will steal moves for scrolling. if you dont need iOS-specific stuff, pointer events (`pointerdown`/`pointermove`, keyed on `pointerId`) do the same thing and cover mouse and pen too.</p>
